## Notes on setting up this blog

Creating new repository and YAML workflow is straightforward, but as I do not yet
know YAML github actions well, this is separate subject to learn.  Jekyll is already set up 
so I choe it for now.

Now to the content.  I choose Creative Commons license, and created a basic README.md.
I test it at https://makefile-blog.github.io/ - it works!

Next step, I got a custom domain (makefile.mk, from North Macedonia, a country I have never
been to.)  I use Fastmail for hosting its DNS, and email (that automatically supports SPF and DMARC).
To support that domain, go to github settings, enable custom domain, then go to DNS panel and add
necessary A and AAAA records to zone apex, or www.${zonename}.  I do the former and add CNAME from 
www.makefile.mk to apex (makefile.mk.)  Both Fastmail and Macedonian registry update domain very fast.

Note that the moment you enable custom domain, your *.github.io site stops working.
Testing http://makefile.mk - it works!

Now for TLS encryption, you want to enable HTTPS and disable HTTP nowaways. This is not the
default for Github pages, so you have to click "enforce HTTPS" in Pages settings but you can 
only do it _after_ SSL certificate is provisioned from Lets Encrypt. This should start automatically,
but takes a bit of time.  I wish there would be possible to click enforce without waiting.

You can search for certificates issued for your domain by visiting https://crt.sh/

Side note: for better decurity, you may wish to add CAA record to your domain, like so:
```
domain.name.local 0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
```

I will let you know how it goes!


